    <body>
      <sec id="article-32900.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Page kidney, also termed Page phenomenon, is a rare and treatable cause of secondary hypertension that results from external compression of the kidney, most commonly due to subcapsular or perinephric blood collections after trauma or iatrogenic procedures. Renal compression produces microvascular ischemia and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, leading to hypertension that may develop rapidly after the inciting event or may present months to years later. Clinical findings are often nonspecific, and physical examination may be unremarkable apart from elevated blood pressure, which can delay diagnosis when a trauma history is absent. Diagnostic evaluation relies on renal imaging, with renal ultrasonography and computed tomography commonly used to identify hematoma, fluid collections, or alternative causes of compression, particularly when predisposing factors are unclear. Management prioritizes blood pressure control with renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system blockade when appropriate and may include percutaneous drainage or surgical decompression for persistent symptoms, enlarging collections, or refractory hypertension.</p>
        <p>Participation in this educational activity strengthens clinicians' ability to recognize Page kidney, select appropriate imaging strategies, and initiate timely medical or procedural treatment to prevent complications such as persistent hypertension, renal impairment, and loss of renal allograft in transplant settings. The activity emphasizes risk assessment in patients with new or refractory hypertension after renal biopsy, renal transplant, shock wave lithotripsy, or abdominal trauma, and highlights differentiating features from other etiologies of elevated renin levels, including renal artery stenosis and parenchymal renal disease. Learners develop skills in coordinating evaluation and treatment across care settings, including consultation with nephrology, urology, radiology, and surgical teams when indicated. Collaboration among the interprofessional health care team improves patient outcomes through coordinated imaging interpretation, safe blood pressure management, appropriate procedural planning, patient counseling about risks and adverse outcomes, and reliable follow-up after intervention.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify patients at risk for Page kidney after renal trauma, renal biopsy, or renal transplant.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess the risk factors for the development of Page kidney.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the appropriate treatment options for Page kidney.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement strategies to improve care coordination among interprofessional team members in order to improve outcomes for patients affected by Page kidney.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=32900&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=32900">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Page kidney, or Page phenomenon, results from external compression of the kidney, primarily from subcapsular or perinephric accumulation of blood, and is a rare, treatable cause of secondary hypertension&#x000a0;that is mediated by activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r1">[1]</xref> The RAAS is activated by microvascular ischemia and can lead to severe hypertension and hypertensive complications. The workup of a young patient with hypertension, defined as high blood pressure in a patient younger than 30, should include investigations to rule out secondary causes. Predisposing factors for Page kidney include recent abdominal procedures, a history of abdominal trauma, underlying malignant neoplasm, uncontrolled hypertension, arteriovenous malformations, and cyst rupture. A basic workup for secondary hypertension often includes renal ultrasonography (particularly in patients younger than 30 years), which is usually effective at&#x000a0;identifying compressive masses but may miss smaller subcapsular collections.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The kidneys are surrounded by a smooth, tough fibrous capsule that limits space; a small amount of fluid under the capsule can compress the kidney parenchyma, resulting in a Page kidney. Gerota fascia, on the other hand, is a larger space and can accommodate considerable blood or fluid before the kidney parenchyma is compressed. In most cases of Page kidney, the compression causing hypertension is extracapsular. (See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Page Kidney, Computed Tomography&#x000a0;Scan.)</p>
        <p>External compression of the kidney may be due to a blood collection or another source. Bleeding around the kidney may result from the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Blunt trauma:</bold>&#x000a0;Sports injuries like football, hockey, other contact sports, motor vehicle accidents, violence, or falls</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Iatrogenic:</bold>&#x000a0;Following biopsy of native or transplant kidney, extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy, ureteral surgical procedure, sympathetic nerve block&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Spontaneous:</bold>&#x000a0;Anticoagulation, arteriovenous malformation, tumors (especially renal cell carcinoma and renal angiomyolipoma), vasculitis, and pancreatitis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cyst rupture:</bold>&#x000a0;Can cause a subcapsular hematoma; while rare, this should be considered in patients with polycystic kidney disease or other known renal cysts&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Nonbleeding causes of external compression include lymphoceles (particularly around the transplanted kidney), urinoma, retroperitoneal paraganglioma, or large simple cysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;The iatrogenic or&#x000a0;traumatic subcapsular hematoma is the most&#x000a0;common cause of Page kidney (See&#x000a0;<bold>Image:</bold>&#x000a0;Renal Hematoma).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Previously, many cases were attributed to sports-related trauma, such as football, or non&#x02013;sports-related blunt trauma of the abdomen, referred to as classic Page kidney.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Assessment&#x000a0;of cases published after 1991 demonstrated an etiological shift toward iatrogenic subcapsular hematoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;These most frequently occurred as complications of renal allograft biopsy, but also occurred after extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy or other procedures such as ureteronephroscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Immediate posttransplant Page kidney has also been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Nontraumatic or spontaneous causes of Page kidney are rare, with isolated cases occasionally reported in the literature. The causes generally include spontaneous renal hemorrhage&#x000a0;because of arteriovenous malformations, underlying tumors, cyst rupture, vasculitis, or glomerulonephritis. Lymphatic collections and subcapsular urinomas have also been observed. Bilateral renal lymphangiectasia has also been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;In rare cases, Page kidney can be idiopathic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Occasionally, spontaneous bilateral Page kidney has been noted in relation to choriocarcinoma metastases, antiplatelet drugs, and pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>In the older literature, the typical patient was a young, healthy man, usually an athlete, who presented with hypertension and who had a history of a sports-related injury. In recent years, the majority of cases have been iatrogenic and related to perirenal procedures or operations, and can occur at all ages. Although the epidemiology is not known, Page kidney should be considered in cases of refractory hypertension, especially in those younger than 30 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Irvine Page first described this entity in 1939. He performed animal experiments in which he wrapped the kidneys in cellophane. An intense inflammatory response followed, producing a fibrocollagenous shell that compressed the kidney. Compression of intrarenal vessels led to ischemia and activation of RAAS. Hypertension developed within 4 to 5 weeks and was cured by nephrectomy of the affected kidney.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;In 1955, Engel and Page described the first case of Page kidney in a 19-year-old football player with a 2-year history of hypertension who had sustained flank trauma 2 years earlier and who was found to have a subcapsular hematoma. Hypertension was cured after nephrectomy.</p>
        <p>Hypertension in the Page kidney is mediated by activation of RAAS, and the mechanism of hypertension is similar to Goldblatt hypertension. Goldblatt, in his experiments, applied clamps to the main renal artery to reduce blood flow. Hypoperfusion of the kidneys leads to the release of renin and activation of RAAS, which leads to hypertension. In the Page kidney, external compression reduces perfusion of intrarenal blood vessels. Resultant microvascular ischemia causes activation of RAAS, leading to hypertension, which can be severe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Proof of RAAS dependence of hypertension in this entity is suggested by&#x000a0;the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated plasma renin activity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated renin level in the venous drainage from the affected kidney</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Normalization of&#x000a0;blood pressure following nephrectomy of the affected kidney</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The presentation of Page kidney may range from a nonspecific&#x000a0;clinical presentation with an insidious onset to a&#x000a0;catastrophic event of hypertensive urgency or emergency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Page kidney&#x000a0;must always be considered in young patients with high blood pressure presenting with a renal mass or a history of abdominal trauma.&#x000a0;Hypertension is the main manifestation of the Page kidney and may develop rapidly after the causative event or slowly, months or years later. This interval could range from days to decades, and a history of trauma may or may not be noted.&#x000a0;In cases of recent perinephric bleeding, a flank hematoma may be seen. Otherwise, physical examination in patients with Page kidney is usually unremarkable, except for hypertension.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Page kidney is most often diagnosed based on imaging studies. Ultrasonography and computed tomography of the kidneys are the most frequently used modalities. Ultrasonography has the advantage of being cheap, quick, easy to perform, easily available, and noninvasive. However, renal ultrasonography can miss small subcapsular hematomas. A CT scan of the abdomen has similar advantages, can detect small hematomas, and is frequently used for follow-up of hemorrhage or if an underlying lesion is suspected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Page kidney without common predisposing factors should prompt further evaluation. As noted, a CT scan may be necessary to evaluate for a suspected underlying lesion. In cases of recurrent spontaneous hemorrhage, angiography may be needed to evaluate for&#x000a0;polyarteritis nodosa, arteriovenous malformations, or renal artery aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Renal function is typically normal in the absence of preexisting renal disease, since the contralateral normal kidney can usually compensate. Renal function may be impaired if the external renal compression is bilateral or there is compression of a single functioning kidney (eg, a transplant kidney). Spontaneous renal hemorrhage should also prompt an evaluation for coagulation disorders. Renal vein renin measurement can certainly suggest activation of RAAS as the causative mechanism of hypertension, but it is usually not performed due to cost, inconvenience, and invasiveness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Previously, radical nephrectomy or open surgical procedures were the mainstay of management of classic Page kidney. With&#x000a0;advances in medical management of hypertension, particularly those targeting&#x000a0;the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system&#x000a0;(RAAS), the management of Page kidney has shifted toward&#x000a0;a more conservative approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0; Activation of RAAS mediates hypertension in this condition, and hence, the ideal agents for the treatment of hypertension are RAAS blockers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r14">[14]</xref> Medical treatment of hypertension may be required temporarily if the renal compression resolves spontaneously or following intervention, for instance, resorption or evacuation of a subcapsular hematoma. Intervention may be needed for larger, symptomatic, or enlarging collections or those with poorly controlled hypertension. The 2 main goals of intervention are renal decompression by evacuating the hematoma and removing the fibrocollagenous pseudocapsule, which can form in chronic cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r18">[18]</xref> Intervention may require open or percutaneous drainage of the compressing collection. If the collection has been long-standing, a fibrocollagenous shell may have formed, which may require decapsulation or even nephrectomy.</p>
        <p>Results from recent case reports have shown that minimally invasive procedures, such as laparoscopy or radiology-assisted percutaneous drainage, are viable therapeutic alternatives.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r9">[9]</xref> Some investigators found that hematoma age was a determining factor in the success of percutaneous drainage. One group found that hematomas less than 3 weeks old usually resolve spontaneously and can be managed medically. However, hematomas older than 3 weeks often develop pseudocapsules that do not respond to percutaneous drainage and require capsulotomy or nephrectomy. Another group suggested that adding urokinase to percutaneous drainage may also be effective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>In&#x000a0;patients with lymphangiomatosis, the preferred management option is percutaneous drainage with sclerosing agent injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Elevation of blood pressure is permanent in some cases, and lifelong antihypertensive drug therapy may be needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r20">[20]</xref> Still, there is no standardized definitive management of Page kidney, as management depends on several patient factors and the underlying pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Absent a history of flank trauma, Page kidney can be difficult to diagnose in a patient presenting with hypertension because it&#x000a0;lacks unique physical examination findings. Imaging is the most appropriate method for diagnosing Page kidney. Elevated renin levels indicate activation of the RAAS as the cause of hypertension and may be due to renal artery stenosis, juxtaglomerular cell tumor, or malignant neoplasm. Additional differential diagnoses include acute rejection, acute tubular necrosis, and renal vein thrombosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The exact percentage of mortality or long-term morbidity is not known, but some cases resolve spontaneously without the need for a surgical procedure. Often, appropriate medical therapy can be a useful temporizing measure because spontaneous resolution may occur later.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Clinicians should be aware of the following complications when evaluating and treating patients with Page kidney:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of renal allograft</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematuria</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Every surgical procedure or interventional investigation comes with a risk of adverse outcomes, and Page kidney is an example of this phenomenon. Patients and their families should be informed in detail by the interdisciplinary team regarding the possible adverse outcomes of undergoing a renal transplant or a renal biopsy.&#x000a0;Patients with Page kidney should receive education to help resolve this complication through medical management. In other cases, a surgical procedure may be needed.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32900.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Providing patient-centered care for individuals with Page kidney&#x000a0;disease requires a collaborative effort among&#x000a0;health care&#x000a0;professionals, including clinicians,&#x000a0; nurses, pharmacists, and others. Primary care clinicians, nephrologists, urologists, surgeons, and radiologists often are part of the interdisciplinary team. Clinicians must possess the necessary clinical skills and expertise to diagnose, evaluate, and treat this condition. Clinical expertise includes proficiency in interpreting radiological findings and recognizing potential complications of procedures. Clinicians should consider urology or general surgery consultation if the subcapsular hemorrhage is significant. Moreover, a strategic approach involving evidence-based guidelines and individualized care plans tailored to each patient's unique circumstances is vital.</p>
        <p>Ethical considerations come into play when determining treatment options and respecting patient autonomy in decision-making. Responsibilities within the interdisciplinary team should be clearly defined, with each member contributing their specialized knowledge and skills to optimize patient care. Effective interdisciplinary communication fosters a collaborative environment where information is shared, questions are encouraged, and concerns are addressed promptly.</p>
        <p>Lastly, care coordination is pivotal to ensuring seamless, efficient patient care. Clinicians,&#x000a0;nurses, pharmacists, and other health care professionals must work together to streamline the patient's journey, from diagnosis through treatment and follow-up. This coordination minimizes errors, reduces delays, and enhances patient safety, ultimately leading to improved outcomes and patient-centered care that prioritizes the well-being and satisfaction of those affected by Page kidney.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32900.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
